      "source_lo": "Student analyses character motivation, dramatic conflict, and thematic resolution by close-reading a play.",
      "source_context": "Shruti's secret fusion rehearsals, Nabin's cold refusal, and his eventual transformation on hearing the music.",
      "item_stem": "Read the following extract from Act III of the play and answer the questions that follow.\n\n1. What does Nabin's physical response — tapping his feet and then clapping furiously — reveal about his internal shift, before he says a single word?\n2. Leela reveals to Shruti, in an aside, that Nabin had once defied his own traditional vocalist household to learn the violin. How does this revelation reframe Nabin's earlier rigidity, and what does it suggest about his admission, 'I underestimated the power of our own music. I was afraid you would be lost to us'?\n3. The play ends with Nabin declaring, 'each bay, its own wind.' Evaluate what this metaphor contributes to the play's resolution of the conflict between tradition and modernity.",
      "question_type": "EXTRACT_ANALYSIS",
      "options": [],
      "visual_stimulus": "Mid-rehearsal, Nabin and Leela arrive unannounced and stand in a corner. The music draws them in; Nabin is soon seen tapping his feet and the act ends with him clapping furiously, his face alight with pride. He praises Shruti specifically for never losing sight of the raga notes, then offers the children his music room for regular practice. In a quiet aside, Leela reveals to Shruti that Nabin himself had once defied his own family — a traditional vocalist household — to take up the violin, a Western instrument. Nabin says: 'I underestimated the power of our own music. I was afraid you would be lost to us.' He then declares his trust in Shruti and his support at the concert, saying: 'each bay, its own wind.'",
      "transcript_ref": "",
      "teacher_guide": {
       "suggested_answer": "",
       "expected_elements": [
        "Physical response (tapping, clapping) shows music moves Nabin before reason does.",
        "Leela's aside reveals Nabin's own past defiance — he, too, broke tradition — making his rigidity ironic.",
        "His admission acknowledges fear of losing Shruti's classical grounding, not rejection of her talent.",
        "'Each bay, its own wind' accepts that each artist's path is individual, resolving the tradition-modernity tension.",
        "Resolution is earned through empathy and the music itself, not through argument or persuasion."
       ],
       "answer_key": [],
       "what_each_option_reveals": {},
       "note": ""
      },
      "verified": true
     }
    ]
